From: Minh Ha-Duong <minh.haduong@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 09:30:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(search): take an item's binary codes with the passages a
 catch-up clears (#30)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

deleteItem drops a whole item's codes before its passages go, because codes are
keyed by rowid and after the delete there is nothing left to name them by. Its
two partial siblings did not. clearFulltext and clearOwnWords remove one item's
body passages, or its notes and annotations, together with the vectors those
passages carry, and left every code taken from them behind.

Those two are the catch-up passes an action:"update" runs. Opening a PDF makes
Zotero extract its text and touches no item version (#26); writing a note
versions the child and not the parent (#33); so these are the only paths that
reach either kind of text, and both replace the item's passages wholesale. One
update over an index that carries codes therefore leaves one orphan code per
replaced passage, permanently: refreshCodes only writes codes for vectors that
have none, so nothing later removes them. loadCodes counts one code per stored
vector, finds more codes than vectors, and declines — every semantic query goes
back to the exact scan the codes exist to avoid, on every query, until someone
runs a full action:"build". Nothing reports it beyond the scan notice on
action:"status", which is why this can sit unnoticed on a working index.

The delete is scoped by source rather than reusing deleteItemCodes. That
statement takes every code of the item, and clearFulltext deliberately leaves
the item's metadata passages in place: stripping their codes would exchange a
surplus for a shortfall and decline the coded path just the same, for rows
nothing touched. It runs before the passages are deleted, not after, because
its subquery names them through `passages` and finds nothing once they are gone.

invalidateCodes() is the second half, and it is not housekeeping: without it a
semantic query that arrives during the catch-up throws. zotero_semantic_search
declines during a job only on an empty index, so on a populated one queries and
an update overlap by design. The pass clears the item's passages, inserts their
replacements with no vector yet, and awaits the embedder. A resident cache that
outlived the clear still names the freed rowid, SQLite has just handed that
rowid back to one of the new rows, and the rescore statement selects candidates
by rowid with no `vector IS NOT NULL` filter — so toFloats dereferences null and
the query fails with "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'byteOffset')".
Dropping the cache is what turns that into the ordinary refusal: no cache, the
job is running, so the query scans exactly and says so.

Three cases, all red before this change: a full-text catch-up and an own-words
catch-up, each asserting that the codes still cover the vectors exactly, that no
code names a passage the index no longer holds, and that the next semantic query
is still served by the codes rather than by the scan; and a query fired from
inside the embedder during a catch-up on the last item indexed, asserting that
it returns its page instead of throwing. The third is red with the two
source-scoped deletes in place and invalidateCodes() alone removed, so both
halves are carrying their own weight.

A fourth case was written and dropped: a catch-up on the last item indexed,
asserting that the replacement does not keep its predecessor's code. It passes
without this change, because putVector resolves a passage id to its rowid and
deletes whatever code sits there before writing the new vector. The rowid-reuse
hazard the deleteItemCodes comment warns about is real for a passage that is
never re-embedded, and that case is caught by the count check like any other
orphan.

